## 5. `01_script/script.md` — the master script

The readable source of truth. Every spoken line is a record. No free prose.

```markdown
# SCRIPT — V001 · <title>
VERSION: v1-estimated · NARRATOR_WPM: 170 (estimated)

## SEG-00 · COLD OPEN · 00:00:00.000 → 00:00:29.800

[L001]
TIME: 00:00:00.000 → 00:00:03.600 · 3.600s · EST
SPEAKER: NARRATOR
VO: VO-001
TEXT: Last night, a sound in the dark made your heart jump.
VISUALS: IMG-001 (L001) · ANM-001 from IMG-002 (L001@"sound")
SFX: SFX-001 (L001@"sound")
TXT: —

[L002]
TIME: 00:00:03.600 → 00:00:05.000 · 1.400s · EST
SPEAKER: NARRATOR
VO: VO-001
TEXT: There was nothing there.
VISUALS: ANM-001 continues
SFX: —
TXT: —

[H001]
TIME: 00:00:05.000 → 00:00:05.600 · 0.600s · EST
TYPE: HOLD
VISUALS: IMG-003 (H001)

[L003]
TIME: 00:00:05.600 → 00:00:07.200 · 1.600s · EST
SPEAKER: NARRATOR
VO: VO-002
...
```

Rules:
- **One narrator line = exactly one sentence** (a fragment counts as a sentence). **One dialogue line = exactly one speaker turn**, at most two short sentences.
- `TEXT` is the script's wording exactly as the script skill wrote it (em dashes and all). The VO files hold the normalized version.
- Every line lists its VO block, every visual that starts inside it, and any SFX or text card anchored in it.
- Segment headers carry the segment's range. They are never spoken.
- End the file with `## FACT NOTES`: the script's verify list and uncertainty note, copied in unchanged.

